<Layout
	title="Editorial Policy — The Wrist Journal"
	description="How The Wrist Journal researches, reviews, recommends, updates, and corrects watch content for Indian readers."
	pubDate={new Date("2026-08-30")}
>
	<p>
		The Wrist Journal is an independent watch publication written and edited by <strong>Arjun Dashrath</strong>. The goal is to make watch coverage more useful for Indian buyers and collectors, especially where global recommendations do not line up neatly with Indian pricing or availability.
	</p>

	<h2>How Recommendations Are Made</h2>

	<p>
		A recommendation has to make sense beyond the spec sheet. I look at the watch itself, its design and movement, the features that matter at that price, competing options, Indian pricing, availability, and whether the overall package feels worth recommending.
	</p>

	<p>
		Price matters, but value is not just about finding the cheapest option. A watch can be worth paying more for if it offers better finishing, a more interesting design, a stronger movement, better durability, or simply a more complete package.
	</p>

	<h2>Research and Sources</h2>

	<p>
		Specifications and factual details are checked against manufacturer information and other reliable sources where needed. Indian pricing and availability can change quickly, so articles may also refer to major retailers, authorised sellers, and current market listings when that context is useful.
	</p>

	<p>
		Prices mentioned on the site should be treated as a snapshot rather than a permanent figure. Discounts, sales, imports, and retailer stock can all change what a watch costs from one week to the next.
	</p>

	<h2>Editorial Independence</h2>

	<p>
		Commercial relationships do not decide what gets recommended. Affiliate links may appear in articles, but they do not change the editorial opinion or the price you pay. If a product or brand relationship ever needs specific disclosure, it will be stated clearly in the relevant article.
	</p>

	<h2>Corrections and Updates</h2>

	<p>
		Watch specifications, prices, and availability can change. When I find a meaningful error or outdated detail, I aim to correct it and update the article where appropriate. If you spot something that needs fixing, email <a href="mailto:arjundashrath@gmail.com">arjundashrath@gmail.com</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>The Standard</h2>

	<p>
		The long-term goal is simple: when The Wrist Journal recommends a watch, I want readers to know that the recommendation was made because I believe the watch deserves it, not because it was the easiest product to link to or the most expensive option on the page.
	</p>
</Layout>
